    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1254">

                <pb facs="1223" n="1057" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg1057"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.63/482</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Ambassador Fletcher </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 377</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-27">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 27, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. Summerlin’s telegram No.
                        342,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1254-f1"/> of July 30, 1917, 4 p.m., reporting
                    that a new mining law containing no radical features was being prepared by the
                    Mexican Minister of Industry and Commerce and would probably be submitted to the
                    Congress at its regular session, you are instructed to furnish the Department
                    with a copy of the new law as soon as it can be obtained.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
